  "worked_solution": "To map the entire region $\u0394$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ and $2\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),\\,2\\le u\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/bfe75ef6b4ea9ddd72be6501f95fd89dd1e80fb2aed691c3c22b158514141cc1.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\n- When $u=2,$ we obtain the point \n$(2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $2.$\n\n- When $u=4,$ we obtain the point \n$(4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $4.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $2$ and $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
